# Source for q/ae-cases.js: Pathology clinical case questions across all pathology topics.
VD="Valve disease"; CM="Cardiomyopathy"; CW="Coronary & wall motion"; PD="Pericardial disease"
CG="Congenital"; MS="Masses"; AO="Aorta"; PH="Pulmonary hypertension"; IE="Endocarditis"
PV="Prosthetic valves"; TD="Transplant & devices"
Q=[
# --- Valve disease cases ---
(VD,"A 78-year-old with exertional chest pain has a calcified aortic valve, peak velocity 4.6 m/s and mean gradient 52 mmHg, EF 60%. This is:","Severe high-gradient aortic stenosis (stage D1)",["Moderate AS","Low-flow, low-gradient AS","Aortic sclerosis"],"Symptoms plus severe AS mean valve replacement is indicated.","C"),
(VD,"A 70-year-old with EF 30% has AVA 0.8 cm² and mean gradient 25 mmHg. On low-dose dobutamine, SV rises 30%, mean gradient rises to 45 mmHg and AVA stays 0.9 cm². This means:","True severe AS with contractile reserve",["Pseudo-severe AS","Mild AS","No flow reserve"],"The valve stays tight as flow rises, confirming severe AS.","C"),
(VD,"A 30-year-old woman from a region with rheumatic fever has dyspnea in pregnancy. Echo shows hockey-stick anterior mitral leaflet, MVA 1.2 cm² and mean gradient 12 mmHg at HR 110. The most likely reason symptoms appeared now is:","Higher heart rate and blood volume in pregnancy raising the MS gradient",["Aortic dissection","New VSD","Pericardial effusion"],"Pregnancy increases cardiac output and shortens diastole.","C"),
(VD,"A patient has a flail P2 scallop with a jet hugging the LA wall, LA very large and systolic reversal in pulmonary veins. The MR is most likely:","Severe primary MR, with the jet directed anteriorly",["Mild MR, directed posteriorly","Functional MR","Rheumatic MR"],"A flail posterior leaflet directs the jet away, toward the anterior LA wall.","C"),
(VD,"A patient with dilated cardiomyopathy has MR with normal leaflets that close below the annulus (tenting) and a central jet. This is:","Secondary (functional) MR, Carpentier type IIIb",["Primary MR type II","Rheumatic MR type IIIa","Endocarditis"],"Treating the LV (medications, CRT) is the first step.","C"),
(VD,"Holodiastolic flow reversal in the abdominal aorta, an AR PHT of 180 ms and a dilated LV. These findings suggest:","Severe chronic aortic regurgitation",["Mild AR","Aortic stenosis","Normal aorta"],"Each finding supports severe AR.","C"),
(VD,"A patient with carcinoid syndrome has thick, stiff tricuspid leaflets fixed open, with a dense triangular TR signal and hepatic vein systolic reversal. The TR is:","Severe",["Mild","Absent","Trace"],"Fixed open leaflets cannot close, causing free-flowing TR.","C"),
(VD,"A young adult has a systolic ejection click and an eccentric closure line of the aortic valve on M-mode. The next most important structure to measure is:","The ascending aorta",["The pulmonary veins","The coronary sinus","The IVC"],"Bicuspid valves are linked with aortopathy.","C"),
(VD,"A patient with a heart murmur has a peak pulmonic valve velocity of 3.5 m/s and doming leaflets. The PS is:","Moderate (peak gradient about 49 mmHg)",["Severe","Mild","None"],"Mild under 3 m/s; moderate 3–4 m/s; severe over 4 m/s.","C"),
(VD,"A 65-year-old with AF has a large LA and annulus, normal leaflets, normal LV size and EF, and moderate central MR. This is most likely:","Atrial functional MR",["Ischemic MR","Rheumatic MR","Flail leaflet"],"LA and annular enlargement pull the leaflets apart.","C"),
(VD,"A dense, early-peaking MR signal and an MR jet that flows along the LA wall into the pulmonary vein in a patient with acute dyspnea after an inferior MI suggest:","Papillary muscle rupture with acute severe MR",["Chronic mild MR","VSD","Normal finding"],"This is a surgical emergency.","C"),
(VD,"A patient with severe AS has LVOT diameter 1.8 cm in PLAX but 3D/CT shows an oval annulus with a larger mean diameter. Using the PLAX value will:","Underestimate AVA by continuity",["Overestimate AVA","Have no effect","Overestimate the gradient"],"The LVOT is often oval, and 2D measures the smaller dimension.","C"),
# --- Cardiomyopathy cases ---
(CM,"A 19-year-old athlete faints while running. Echo shows a septum of 22 mm, posterior wall 11 mm and SAM with a resting gradient of 60 mmHg. The diagnosis is:","Obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy",["Athlete's heart","Hypertensive heart disease","Amyloidosis"],"Exertional syncope is a red flag for sudden death risk.","C"),
(CM,"A 75-year-old man with carpal tunnel syndrome has thick LV walls, low voltage ECG, biatrial enlargement, and apical sparing strain. The most likely diagnosis is:","Transthyretin (ATTR) cardiac amyloidosis",["HCM","Athlete's heart","Constrictive pericarditis"],"Carpal tunnel often precedes ATTR heart involvement.","C"),
(CM,"A woman develops heart failure 3 weeks after giving birth. Echo shows a dilated LV with EF 30%. This is most likely:","Peripartum cardiomyopathy",["Takotsubo","HCM","Constriction"],"Many recover, but future pregnancies carry risk.","C"),
(CM,"A 25-year-old with ventricular tachycardia has a dilated RV with an aneurysm of the RV free wall and normal LV. Consider:","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy",["Ebstein anomaly","ASD","Pulmonary stenosis"],"Family screening is important.","C"),
(CM,"A patient with heart failure has deep LV trabeculations with a noncompacted/compacted ratio of 2.5 in systole and color flow in the recesses. The finding suggests:","LV noncompaction",["Apical thrombus","Myxoma","HCM"],"Thrombus risk is increased; contrast helps confirm.","C"),
(CM,"After breast cancer treatment with doxorubicin and trastuzumab, a patient's GLS drops from −21% to −16%, but EF stays at 58%. This suggests:","Subclinical cardiotoxicity",["Normal variation","Improved function","Constrictive pericarditis"],"A relative drop over 15% is significant; the oncology team should be told.","C"),
(CM,"A young man has a dilated LV with EF 25% and a history of very heavy alcohol use. The best explanation is:","Alcoholic dilated cardiomyopathy, which may improve with abstinence",["HCM","Amyloidosis","Constriction"],"Stopping alcohol can lead to recovery.","C"),
(CM,"A patient's LV EF improves from 25% to 55% after heart rate control of a long-standing fast AF. This suggests:","Tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy",["Amyloidosis","Ischemic scar","ARVC"],"Recovery after rate control is the key clue.","C"),
# --- Coronary cases ---
(CW,"A 60-year-old with chest pain has akinesis of the anterior wall, anteroseptum and apex, and an apical thrombus. The culprit artery is most likely the:","Proximal LAD",["RCA","LCx","Posterior descending artery"],"Large anterior-apical MIs carry the highest thrombus risk.","C"),
(CW,"After an inferior MI, a patient has hypotension, raised neck veins and clear lungs. Echo shows a dilated, hypokinetic RV. The best explanation is:","Right ventricular infarction",["Tamponade","LV free wall rupture","Aortic dissection"],"Give fluids; avoid nitrates.","C"),
(CW,"Five days after a large anterior MI, a new loud murmur appears. Color shows a left-to-right jet through the apical septum. This is:","Post-MI ventricular septal rupture",["Papillary muscle rupture","Mitral prolapse","Pericarditis"],"Emergency repair is often needed.","C"),
(CW,"A 70-year-old woman with chest pain after her husband's funeral has apical ballooning, basal hyperkinesis and normal coronaries. This is:","Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy",["Anterior STEMI","HCM","Myocarditis"],"Function usually recovers within weeks.","C"),
(CW,"An inferolateral wall is thin (5 mm), bright and akinetic, with no improvement at low-dose dobutamine. This segment is:","Scar, unlikely to recover after revascularization",["Hibernating","Stunned","Normal"],"Viable segments thicken at low dose.","C"),
(CW,"A patient weeks after an MI has a wide-necked bulge at the apex that moves outward in systole and has a thin wall of myocardium. This is:","A true LV aneurysm",["A pseudoaneurysm","A diverticulum","Normal apex"],"Pseudoaneurysms have narrow necks and no myocardium in the wall.","C"),
(CW,"During dobutamine stress, the inferior and inferolateral walls become akinetic while the anterior wall becomes hyperkinetic. This suggests ischemia in the territory of the:","RCA and/or LCx",["LAD","Left main only","Diagonal branch"],"The anterior wall's normal response shows the LAD is likely fine.","C"),
(CW,"A young woman 2 weeks after delivery has chest pain and anterior wall hypokinesis. Angiography shows a coronary dissection. This is:","Spontaneous coronary artery dissection (SCAD)",["Takotsubo","Peripartum cardiomyopathy","Pulmonary embolism"],"SCAD is a common cause of MI around pregnancy.","C"),
# --- Pericardial cases ---
(PD,"After cardiac surgery, a patient becomes hypotensive. TTE shows a small anterior effusion but a large clot is seen behind the LA on TEE. The diagnosis is:","Localized (loculated) tamponade",["Pleural effusion","Normal postoperative finding","Constrictive pericarditis"],"Typical tamponade signs may be absent with localized clot.","C"),
(PD,"A patient with prior chest radiation has dyspnea, a septal bounce, mitral E variation of 35%, medial e′ of 12 cm/s and expiratory hepatic vein diastolic reversal. The diagnosis is:","Constrictive pericarditis",["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Tamponade","Normal heart"],"Preserved medial e′ points away from restriction.","C"),
(PD,"A patient with fever and pleuritic chest pain relieved by leaning forward has diffuse ST elevation and a small effusion. The likely diagnosis is:","Acute pericarditis",["STEMI","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism"],"Echo is often normal or shows a small effusion.","C"),
(PD,"A patient from a TB-endemic area has a large effusion with fibrin strands and thickened pericardium. The most likely cause is:","Tuberculous pericarditis",["Viral pericarditis","Hypothyroidism","Uremia"],"TB is a leading cause of effusion and constriction in many countries.","C"),
(PD,"A large effusion with RA collapse for more than one-third of systole, RV diastolic collapse, IVC 2.5 cm without collapse and mitral E falling 40% with inspiration. This is:","Cardiac tamponade",["A small effusion","Constriction","Pleural effusion"],"Urgent drainage is usually needed.","C"),
(PD,"In a patient with severe pulmonary hypertension, a large effusion does not cause RV collapse because:","The thick, high-pressure RV resists collapse",["The effusion is small","Tamponade is impossible","The LA is large"],"Tamponade can develop without RV collapse in PH.","C"),
(PD,"A patient on dialysis with chest pain has a moderate effusion. The most likely cause is:","Uremic pericarditis",["Takotsubo","Endocarditis","Myxoma"],"Better dialysis often treats it.","C"),
# --- Congenital cases ---
(CG,"A 40-year-old with palpitations has a dilated RV, paradoxical septal motion and a 2 cm defect in the middle of the atrial septum. Qp/Qs is 2.0. This is:","A significant secundum ASD",["A PFO","A primum ASD","A sinus venosus ASD"],"Device closure is often possible with adequate rims.","C"),
(CG,"An adult with an unexplained dilated RV has a normal-looking atrial septum on TTE. The next step should be:","Look for a sinus venosus ASD or anomalous pulmonary veins, often with TEE or MRI",["Report a normal study","Close the case","Exercise testing only"],"Sinus venosus defects are hard to see on TTE.","C"),
(CG,"A patient with Down syndrome has a defect in the lower atrial septum, a cleft mitral leaflet and the AV valves at the same level. This is:","A partial atrioventricular canal (primum ASD)",["Secundum ASD","Tetralogy of Fallot","VSD only"],"Loss of the normal tricuspid offset is a key clue.","C"),
(CG,"An adult with repaired tetralogy of Fallot has free pulmonary regurgitation and a dilated RV. The main future intervention to consider is:","Pulmonary valve replacement",["Mitral valve repair","Aortic valve replacement","Pacemaker only"],"Timing depends on RV size, function and symptoms.","C"),
(CG,"A young man with hypertension has weak femoral pulses. Suprasternal CW shows 3.5 m/s with a diastolic tail. The most likely diagnosis is:","Coarctation of the aorta",["Aortic stenosis","PDA","Renal artery stenosis only"],"Look for an associated bicuspid aortic valve.","C"),
(CG,"A patient with cyanosis and clubbing has a large VSD with low-velocity bidirectional flow and severe PH. This is:","Eisenmenger syndrome",["Small restrictive VSD","Normal heart","Tetralogy of Fallot only"],"Closure of the defect is not recommended.","C"),
(CG,"A woman has a continuous murmur under the left clavicle and a dilated LA and LV. Color shows flow into the main PA near its bifurcation. The diagnosis is:","Patent ductus arteriosus",["ASD","VSD","Coarctation"],"A large PDA causes left heart volume overload.","C"),
# --- Masses cases ---
(MS,"A 50-year-old woman with fatigue and a 'tumor plop' has a mobile mass attached to the fossa ovalis, prolapsing through the mitral valve. The diagnosis is:","Left atrial myxoma",["Fibroelastoma","LA thrombus","Rhabdomyoma"],"Surgical removal is needed.","C"),
(MS,"An elderly patient with a stroke has a 7 mm mobile mass with frond-like edges on the aortic side of the aortic valve, no fever and negative cultures. Most likely:","Papillary fibroelastoma",["Vegetation","Myxoma","Lambl's excrescence"],"Surgery is often advised because of stroke risk.","C"),
(MS,"A patient with AF and mitral stenosis has a mass in the LA appendage on TEE. Most likely:","Thrombus",["Myxoma","Lipoma","Fibroma"],"Slow flow in the appendage favors clot.","C"),
(MS,"A patient with kidney cancer has a mass extending from the IVC into the RA. The most likely explanation is:","Tumor extension through the IVC from renal cell carcinoma",["A myxoma","A Chiari network","A thrombus from the LA"],"Follow the IVC from the subcostal view.","C"),
(MS,"An infant with seizures and skin lesions has several bright masses in both ventricles. The most likely diagnosis is:","Rhabdomyomas with tuberous sclerosis",["Myxomas","Fibroelastomas","Angiosarcoma"],"Many shrink on their own.","C"),
# --- Aorta cases ---
(AO,"A 55-year-old with sudden tearing chest pain to the back has a mobile linear flap in the ascending aorta, new AR and a pericardial effusion. This is:","Type A aortic dissection, a surgical emergency",["Type B dissection","Aortic aneurysm only","Acute pericarditis"],"Tell the physician immediately.","C"),
(AO,"A tall 22-year-old with lens dislocation has an aortic root of 4.7 cm with effaced sinotubular junction. The diagnosis is most likely:","Marfan syndrome with aortic root aneurysm",["Normal aorta","Coarctation","Takayasu arteritis"],"Surgery is often advised around 5.0 cm in Marfan syndrome.","C"),
(AO,"A patient after a high-speed car crash has a widened mediastinum. TEE shows a flap just beyond the left subclavian artery. The likely diagnosis is:","Traumatic aortic injury at the isthmus",["Type A dissection","Coarctation","Aortic stenosis"],"Deceleration injuries tear the aorta at this fixed point.","C"),
(AO,"A patient with a bicuspid aortic valve has an ascending aorta of 4.8 cm with a normal root. This pattern is:","Bicuspid aortopathy, which often affects the ascending aorta",["Marfan syndrome","Normal size","Coarctation"],"Follow it regularly with echo, CT or MRI.","C"),
(AO,"A patient with an ascending aortic aneurysm and AR has a positive syphilis test. The aortic finding may be from:","Syphilitic aortitis",["Takayasu","Marfan","Coarctation"],"It typically affects the ascending aorta.","C"),
(AO,"A 68-year-old smoker has an abdominal aortic diameter of 5.8 cm. This is:","A large AAA that usually needs repair",["Normal size","A small aneurysm needing no follow-up","Coarctation"],"Repair is often advised at about 5.5 cm in men.","C"),
# --- Pulmonary hypertension cases ---
(PH,"A 35-year-old woman with dyspnea has TR velocity 4.2 m/s, D-shaped septum, RA 22 cm² and normal LV and LA. Most likely:","Pre-capillary pulmonary arterial hypertension, needing right heart catheterization",["Left heart disease","Normal heart","Constriction"],"Normal left heart findings point away from group 2.","C"),
(PH,"A patient with COPD has a TR velocity of 3.3 m/s, a mildly dilated RV and normal left heart. The most likely PH group is:","Group 3 (lung disease or hypoxia)",["Group 1","Group 2","Group 5"],"Treating the lung disease and oxygen are key.","C"),
(PH,"A patient with a previous PE has persistent dyspnea months later, a dilated RV and TR velocity 3.8 m/s. The next test to look for CTEPH is:","A V/Q lung scan",["An ECG","Exercise treadmill only","A TEE"],"CTEPH can be cured with surgery in many cases.","C"),
(PH,"A patient with acute PE has a dilated RV, RV/LV ratio 1.3, McConnell's sign and TR velocity of 2.6 m/s. The low TR velocity is because:","The acutely failing, thin RV cannot generate high pressure",["PA pressure is normal","The TR is severe and reflects only flow","PH is chronic"],"Chronic PH usually has higher TR velocities and a thick RV.","C"),
(PH,"A patient with severe MS has TR velocity 3.6 m/s. The PH is most likely:","Group 2 (left heart disease)",["Group 1","Group 3","Group 4"],"Raised LA pressure passes backward to the lungs.","C"),
# --- Endocarditis, prosthetic, devices ---
(IE,"A patient who injects drugs has fever and a 15 mm mobile mass on the tricuspid valve with severe TR. Lung CT shows multiple nodules. The diagnosis is:","Tricuspid endocarditis with septic pulmonary emboli",["Myxoma","Carcinoid","Lambl's excrescence"],"S. aureus is the most common cause.","C"),
(IE,"A patient with aortic valve endocarditis develops new first-degree heart block and an echo-free space next to the aortic root. Most likely:","Aortic root abscess",["Pericardial cyst","Normal finding","Coronary aneurysm"],"This needs urgent surgery.","C"),
(IE,"A patient with a pacemaker has fever and positive blood cultures. TEE shows a mobile mass on the RV lead. The treatment is:","Complete removal of the device system plus antibiotics",["Antibiotics only","Leaving the device","Pericardiocentesis"],"A new device is placed after the infection clears.","C"),
(PV,"Two years after mechanical mitral valve replacement, a patient with low INR has dyspnea, a mean gradient of 14 mmHg and one leaflet not moving. Most likely:","Prosthetic valve thrombosis",["Normal function","Pannus only","Paravalvular leak"],"Recent subtherapeutic anticoagulation favors thrombus.","C"),
(PV,"Ten years after a bioprosthetic aortic valve, a patient has a mean gradient rising from 12 to 38 mmHg with thick, calcified leaflets. This is:","Structural valve deterioration",["Prosthesis-patient mismatch from the start","Normal aging of a mechanical valve","Endocarditis always"],"Valve-in-valve TAVR may be an option.","C"),
(PV,"A patient with a small surgical aortic bioprosthesis has a high gradient from day one with normal leaflet motion and an indexed EOA of 0.6 cm²/m². This is:","Severe prosthesis–patient mismatch",["Prosthetic thrombosis","Structural deterioration","Paravalvular leak"],"The valve works normally but is too small for the patient.","C"),
(TD,"A patient with an LVAD has low pump flow. Echo shows a small LV, septum bowed to the left and the inflow cannula against the septum. The likely problem is:","A suction event from pump speed set too high or low volume",["Pump thrombosis with a dilated LV","Aortic stenosis","Normal finding"],"Reducing speed or giving fluid may help.","C"),
(TD,"A heart transplant patient has a new pericardial effusion, thicker LV walls and a restrictive filling pattern compared with prior studies. Consider:","Acute rejection",["Normal recovery","Coarctation","Mitral stenosis"],"Biopsy confirms the diagnosis.","C"),
]
